# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
james-will-2.md:1: argue/readings: S2 is read 2 ways: james-will-2.logic.r2 has "world_contrivance ∧ ¬world_machine → acts_good ∧ regret_good" and "acts_good ∧ regret_good → world_contrivance ∧ ¬world_machine"; james-will-2.logic.r2.2 and james-will-2.logic.r2.3 have "¬world_machine ∧ world_contrivance → acts_good ∧ regret_good"; they differ when only "the necessary acts we erroneously regret may be good", "our error in so regretting them may be also good", "the world is regarded as a contrivance for deepening the theoretic consciousness of what goodness and evil in their intrinsic natures are" and "the world is regarded as a machine whose final purpose is the making real of any outward good" are true, which the second allows
james-will-2.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered no, which keeps james-will-2.logic.r2, to: Suppose "the necessary acts we erroneously regret may be good", "our error in so regretting them may be also good", "the world is regarded as a contrivance for deepening the theoretic consciousness of what goodness and evil in their intrinsic natures are" and "the world is regarded as a machine whose final purpose is the making real of any outward good" are true. Is S2, "The necessary acts we erroneously regret {165} may be good, and yet our error in so regretting them may be also good, on one simple condition; and that condition is this: The world must not be regarded as a machine whose final purpose is the making real of any outward good, but rather as a contrivance for deepening the theoretic consciousness of what goodness and evil in their intrinsic natures are.", true then?
james-will-2.md:1: argue/readings: S6 is read 3 ways: james-will-2.logic.r2 has "nature_knowing → ¬optimism ∧ ¬pessimism"; james-will-2.logic.r2.2 has "¬optimism ∧ ¬pessimism"; they differ when only "the world is an optimism" is true, which the first allows; james-will-2.logic.r2.3 has "world_contrivance → ¬optimism ∧ ¬pessimism"
james-will-2.md:1: argue/answered: S6: answered no, which keeps james-will-2.logic.r2.2 and james-will-2.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "the world is regarded as a contrivance for deepening the theoretic consciousness of what goodness and evil in their intrinsic natures are" and "the world is an optimism" are true and "not the doing either of good or of evil is what nature cares for, but the knowing of them" and "the world is a pessimism" are false. Is S6, "According to it, the world is neither an optimism nor a pessimism, but a gnosticism.", true then?
james-will-2.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: james-will-2.logic.r2 and james-will-2.logic.r2.2 have "nature_knowing → world_contrivance ∧ ¬world_machine" and "acts_good ∧ regret_good → refuge"; james-will-2.logic.r2.3 has "nature_knowing ∧ life_knowledge → world_contrivance ∧ ¬world_machine" and "acts_good ∧ regret_good → refuge"; they differ when only "the necessary acts we erroneously regret may be good", "our error in so regretting them may be also good", "the world is regarded as a contrivance for deepening the theoretic consciousness of what goodness and evil in their intrinsic natures are", "the world is regarded as a machine whose final purpose is the making real of any outward good", "not the doing either of good or of evil is what nature cares for, but the knowing of them" and "the refuge from the quandary lies not far off" are true, which the second allows
james-will-2.md:1: argue/unfaithful: james-will-2.logic.r2 P5 (S6): the sentence has no word of "not the doing either of good or of evil is what nature cares for, but the knowing of them": is it the sentence P5 came from?
